@ECHO OFF
REM ===================================================================
REM  Suggested AUTOEXEC.BAT for a dosbridge client.
REM
REM  This is a SUGGESTION, not a drop-in replacement. Merge the parts
REM  you need into your own AUTOEXEC.BAT. Keep a backup first:
REM      COPY C:\AUTOEXEC.BAT C:\AUTOEXEC.SAV
REM ===================================================================

REM C:\TOOLS must be on PATH or the tools will not resolve by name.
PATH=C:\AI;C:\AGENT;C:\TOOLS;C:\WORK;C:\DOS;C:\MTCP

SET MTCPCFG=C:\MTCP\MTCP.CFG
PROMPT $P$G

REM --- network, before anything that can wedge the machine ------------
REM This ordering is load-bearing. The crash guard in AI.BAT can only
REM phone home about a driver that killed the previous boot if the
REM network is already up when it runs.
REM
REM Use whichever packet driver matches your card, and match the
REM IRQ/port to its jumpers or its own config:
REM
REM   PicoMEM :  C:\DRIVERS\PM2000.COM 0x60
REM   NE2000  :  NE2000.COM 0x60 3 0x300
REM
NE2000.COM 0x60 3 0x300

REM --- DO NOT RUN DHCP -----------------------------------------------
REM DHCP.EXE is one-shot. It stamps IPADDR, TIMESTAMP and LEASE_TIME
REM into MTCP.CFG and exits, and nothing renews the lease. When it
REM expires every mTCP tool refuses to run and the box goes silent --
REM and it cannot recover on its own, because the :OFFLINE branch in
REM AI.BAT retries HTGET forever but never re-runs DHCP. That costs you
REM a walk to the machine.
REM
REM Set a static IPADDR/NETMASK/GATEWAY in MTCP.CFG instead. It also
REM saves several seconds on every boot, which adds up on a machine
REM that reboots as often as this one does.
REM
REM DHCP

REM --- hand off to the agent loop -------------------------------------
CD \AI
CALL AI.BAT
